= Self-organized criticality — Simple =

'''Self-organized criticality''' (SOC) is the idea that some slowly driven systems can organize themselves into a critical-like state where events occur across many different sizes.

The classic example is a mathematical sandpile.

Grains are added slowly.

When one site becomes too high, it topples and can trigger neighboring sites.

One added grain can therefore cause:

* no avalanche;
* a tiny avalanche;
* a very large avalanche.

Per Bak, Chao Tang, and Kurt Wiesenfeld introduced the SOC idea in 1987 and 1988.<ref name="btw">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.381 Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld (1987), “Self-Organized Criticality.”]</ref>

== Criticality ==

At a critical point, systems can show behavior over many scales instead of one typical scale.

Ordinary critical points often require tuning a parameter such as temperature.

SOC proposes that some driven and dissipative systems can move toward a critical-like state through their own dynamics.

== Slow driving and fast avalanches ==

In the standard sandpile model, one grain is added only after the previous avalanche has stopped.

That creates two time scales:

* slow driving;
* fast relaxation.

This separation is an important part of the classic model.

Real systems do not always satisfy it.

== Power laws ==

Avalanche sizes are often tested for a power-law-like distribution:

<math>P(s)\propto s^{-\tau}.</math>

This means small events are common and large events are rarer, without one obvious typical avalanche size over the scaling range.

But a power law alone does not prove SOC.

Other processes can also produce heavy tails.

== Real sand is not the mathematical sandpile ==

Real granular material depends on:

* friction;
* grain shape;
* packing;
* inertia.

Experiments with rice found SOC-like behavior for elongated grains but not for less elongated grains.<ref name="rice">[https://doi.org/10.1038/379049a0 Frette et al. (1996), “Avalanche dynamics in a pile of rice.”]</ref>

This shows that details of the physical system matter.

== What should researchers test? ==

A stronger SOC claim checks:

* slow drive and dissipation;
* stationarity;
* event-size scaling;
* several system sizes;
* finite-size effects;
* alternative statistical models.

A straight-looking line on a log-log graph is not enough.

== <math>1/f</math> noise ==

The original papers connected SOC with <math>1/f</math>-type noise.

But many mechanisms can generate similar spectra.

Seeing <math>1/f</math> noise is therefore not proof of SOC.

== Other applications ==

SOC ideas have been applied to:

* earthquakes;
* solar flares;
* neural activity;
* ecology;
* social systems.

These applications need mechanism-specific evidence.

An “avalanche-like” pattern in data does not automatically mean the underlying process is a sandpile-like self-organized critical system.

== SOC and self-organization ==

[[Self-organization]] is broader.

A system can self-organize without being near a critical point.

SOC is one particular proposed form of self-organization.

== SOC and emergence ==

[[Emergence]] is also broader.

A system can have emergent patterns without scale-free critical behavior.

